## codehud (system HUD)

This repo has a system map at `.codehud/system.json` and the `codehud` MCP server is available. The human
watches a browser HUD of the system while you work in the terminal. Use it:

- At the start of a task, call `hud_get_map` once so you know the node ids and which files belong to which node.
- Whenever you move to a different part of the system (reading, editing, testing), call `hud_focus` with the
  node ids or file paths and a one-line `note` of what you are doing there. Cheap; call it liberally.
- When you need a decision from the human about the system, call `hud_ask` instead of asking in plain text.
  Pass the `nodes`/`files` the question is about, a short `context`, a `moreContext` with your reasoning,
  trade-offs and any snippet or diff, and `options` when the choice is finite. Wait for the answer. If the
  call times out, call `hud_await` with the question id, or proceed with your best judgement and say so.
- Post short progress notes with `hud_note` at milestones (tests green, migration written, moving on).
- If you change the system's shape (new route, table, service, external dependency), update the system map
  (re-run the crawler or edit `.codehud/system.json`) and call `hud_load_map`.

Do not ask questions in the HUD that are not about the system (e.g. "shall I continue?"); keep those in the terminal.
